    "lawCode": "Amd §16-v, UDC Act",
    "lawSection": "New York State Urban Development Corporation Act",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7961\n\nSPONSOR: RANZENHOFER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the urban development corporation act,\nin relation to the New York state business incubator and innovation hot\nspot support act\n\n \nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nThe proposed legislation amends the Incubator and Innovation Hot Spot\nprogram to clarify language in the statute as it is written and provide\nan environment that strengthens the program and fosters collaboration\namongst the Hot Spots and Incubators.  Summary of Provisions:\n\nSection one of the proposed legislation amends paragraph (c) of\nsubsection 2 of section 16-v of the Urban Development Corporation Act to\nclarify the designation of ten Hot Spots is not limited to a certain\ntimeframe.\n\nSection two of this legislation amends paragraph (c) of subsection 5 of\nsection 16-v of the Urban Development Corporation Act to only exclude\nindirect costs from acceptable expenses and require that at least eighty\nfive percent of all funds go towards operations for sponsored incuba-\ntors.\n\nSection three of the proposed legislation amends subsection 6 of section\n16-v of the Urban Development Corporation Act to clarify that ESD making\nother assistance available as appropriate to designees is discretionary\nand not a mandatory requirement of the law.\n\nSection four of the proposed legislation amends the Urban Development\nCorporation Act to allow Hot Spots to enter into a memorandum of under-\nstanding with Incubators to provide tax benefits or provide services or\nassistance to clients in addition to what is provided by the Incubator\nwithout risk of the companies losing the tax benefits.\n\nSection five of the proposed legislation provides that the proposed\nlegislation is to take effect immediately upon the effective date of the\nbill.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis proposed legislative change would strengthen the NYS Certified\nBusiness Incubator and Innovation Hot Spot networks and leverage state\nfunding to expand services to a greater number of entrepreneurs and\nstartup companies. The statute, as it stands, creates competition among\nthe Innovation Hot Spots and NYS Certified Incubators instead of foster-\ning collaboration. Because current law can be interpreted to provide\nthat any company that works with both an incubator and a Hot Spot is not\neligible for the tax benefit, it is important to clarify that an early\nstage company working with both an incubator and Hot Spot can still\nrealize the tax benefit through the Hot Spot if an MOU is in place\nbetween both entities.\n\nESD is confident that the proposed changes would not only be beneficial\nfor the business entities but for the overall entrepreneurial ecosystem\nof the State of New York.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill would take effect immediately.\n",
